# trios-trainer-igla corpus manifest
#
# The corpora themselves are NOT in git (see .gitignore: /data/*). This file is
# how a third party confirms they are holding the same bytes this repository's
# checkpoints were measured against. Every line below was produced by running
# `shasum -a 256` on this machine on 2026-08-03; nothing here is copied from a
# log or from another document.
#
# Verify (from the repository root):
#
#   shasum -a 256 -c data/MANIFEST.sha256
#
# Byte counts, for the same files, in the same order:
#
#   data/tiny_shakespeare.txt      1015394 bytes
#   data/tiny_shakespeare_val.txt   100000 bytes
#
# The two streams are byte-DISJOINT. Concatenated in the order train then val
# they reconstruct the canonical 1 115 394-byte tinyshakespeare corpus:
#
#   cat data/tiny_shakespeare.txt data/tiny_shakespeare_val.txt | shasum -a 256
#   sha256 = 86c4e6aa9db7c042ec79f339dcb96d42b0075e16b8fc2e86bf0ca57e2dc565ed
#   bytes  = 1115394
#
# That concatenation hash is the evidence that the split is a partition and not
# a copy. An earlier README documented the split as `head -c 100000 train > val`,
# which is a LEAKING split; the Dockerfile had already been fixed, the prose had
# not. A hash of the union is checkable, a sentence about a shell command is not.
#
1a5aead1db78653f48ee799c4145ef71265f6aadd2c79ebffc9f0260cac1fb0d  data/tiny_shakespeare.txt
2088af36b1c7831083ef22c0f6e1999b1dece15b9fbe2d4695364e95d497d502  data/tiny_shakespeare_val.txt
#